std.cmp — Eq and Ord traits
============================

The `std.cmp` module provides the canonical `Eq[T]` and
`Ord[T]` trait declarations. User-defined struct and enum
types implement these traits to participate in operator
overloading (`==`, `!=`, `<`, `>`, `<=`, `>=`) and in
generic functions bounded by `Eq` or `Ord`.

Primitive types (`int`, `i64`, `byte`, `bool`, `double`,
`string`) have built-in equality and comparison that works
without these traits. `Eq[T]` and `Ord[T]` extend that
capability to user-defined types.

Dependencies: none.


    module std.cmp


Eq
--

`Eq[T]` requires a single method `eq(a, b) -> bool`.
The default method `ne` is provided automatically.

When a type implements `Eq[T]`, the operators `==` and `!=`
dispatch through `eq` and `ne` respectively for values of
that type.

    trait Eq[T]
        eq(a: T, b: T) -> bool
        ne(a: T, b: T) -> bool = !eq(a, b)


Ord
---

`Ord[T]` requires a single method `cmp(a, b) -> int`:

- negative → `a` before `b`
- zero → `a` equal to `b`
- positive → `a` after `b`

Default methods derive `lt`, `le`, `gt`, `ge` from `cmp`.
When a type implements `Ord[T]`, the operators `<`, `>`,
`<=`, `>=` dispatch through these methods.

    trait Ord[T]
        cmp(a: T, b: T) -> int
        lt(a: T, b: T) -> bool = cmp(a, b) < 0
        le(a: T, b: T) -> bool = cmp(a, b) <= 0
        gt(a: T, b: T) -> bool = cmp(a, b) > 0
        ge(a: T, b: T) -> bool = cmp(a, b) >= 0


Tests
=====

Tests exercise `Eq` and `Ord` on a simple user-defined struct.

    struct Point
        x: int
        y: int

    impl Eq[Point]
        eq(a: Point, b: Point) -> bool = a.x == b.x && a.y == b.y

    impl Ord[Point]
        cmp(a: Point, b: Point) -> int
            if a.x != b.x then return a.x - b.x
            a.y - b.y


### Eq

    #test
    test_eq_same() -> unit
        var a: Point
        a.x = 1
        a.y = 2
        var b: Point
        b.x = 1
        b.y = 2
        assert(a == b, "equal")

    #test
    test_eq_different() -> unit
        var a: Point
        a.x = 1
        a.y = 2
        var b: Point
        b.x = 1
        b.y = 3
        assert(a != b, "not equal")

    #test
    test_ne_is_derived() -> unit
        var a: Point
        a.x = 1
        a.y = 2
        var b: Point
        b.x = 3
        b.y = 4
        assert(Eq.ne(a, b), "ne works via default method")


### Ord

    #test
    test_ord_less() -> unit
        var a: Point
        a.x = 1
        a.y = 0
        var b: Point
        b.x = 2
        b.y = 0
        assert(a < b, "1 < 2")

    #test
    test_ord_greater() -> unit
        var a: Point
        a.x = 3
        a.y = 0
        var b: Point
        b.x = 1
        b.y = 0
        assert(a > b, "3 > 1")

    #test
    test_ord_equal() -> unit
        var a: Point
        a.x = 1
        a.y = 2
        var b: Point
        b.x = 1
        b.y = 2
        assert(a <= b, "le")
        assert(a >= b, "ge")
        assert(!(a < b), "not lt")
        assert(!(a > b), "not gt")

    #test
    test_ord_secondary_key() -> unit
        var a: Point
        a.x = 1
        a.y = 2
        var b: Point
        b.x = 1
        b.y = 5
        assert(a < b, "same x, compare y")

    #test
    test_ord_default_methods() -> unit
        var a: Point
        a.x = 1
        a.y = 0
        var b: Point
        b.x = 2
        b.y = 0
        assert(Ord.lt(a, b), "lt via trait call")
        assert(Ord.le(a, b), "le via trait call")
        assert(!Ord.gt(a, b), "not gt via trait call")
        assert(!Ord.ge(a, b), "not ge via trait call")


Design notes
------------

**Minimal trait surface.** `Eq` requires only `eq`; `Ord`
requires only `cmp`. Everything else is a default method.
This matches Rust's `PartialEq`/`Ord` in spirit, with one
simplification: Sysl doesn't distinguish `PartialEq` from
`Eq` (no NaN-aware partial equality), because `Eq` is the
common case and partial ordering is a niche concern.

**No impls for primitives.** `int == int` and `int < int`
work via built-in operators, not through these traits.
Trait-based dispatch applies only to user-defined types that
explicitly `impl Eq[T]` or `impl Ord[T]`. When trait bounds
are used in generic functions (`fn f[T: Ord]`), the bound is
checked at instantiation time: primitive types satisfy it via
built-in operators, user types via their trait impl.

**Operator desugaring.** When the analyzer sees `a < b` where
`a` and `b` are a struct type with `impl Ord[T]`, it rewrites
to `Ord.lt(a, b)`. This is transparent to the user — they
write operators and the compiler routes to trait methods.
